Systems Engineering Handbook — NASA (@mech-nasa-systems: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mech-sources) { r_7ZQFZ9E1NAVG27SE2R5S24PDBQ
NASA, *Systems Engineering Handbook*, NASA/SP-2016-6105 Rev 2. [Official PDF](https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/nasa_systems_engineering_handbook_0.pdf), 297 pages. The report identifier is 2016; the publication record was released in 2017.

Read chapters 2 and 4–5, then relevant technical-management sections in chapter 6, to connect requirements, implementation, integration, verification, validation and transition. Apply the reasoning to the actual machine; institutional forms and organizational scale are not prerequisites for creating a Record or following this route.

The contents, introductory material and selected requirements/acceptance passages were inspected on 30 October 2026. This is publicly available US-government guidance, with any credited third-party components subject to their own notices. Do not assume every illustration shares the status of government-authored text. The historical handbook does not by itself establish current requirements for another organization. Keep its PDF external.

**Return:** [Mechanical-engineering sources](../Sources.lingua#r_06RZYPZEFNMZ6MZ690QB1WN1Z2).
The clean-manufacturing qualification section additionally inspected the official [Product Realization web chapter](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/5-0-product-realization/), particularly integration, verification and validation passages. It applies the reasoning about configuration, evidence and intended use to benign manufactured objects. NASA flight-unit qualification terminology and institutional procedures are not asserted as universal industrial requirements.


**Common engineering inspection — 31 October 2026:** the official [System Design Processes](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/4-0-system-design-processes/) passages on stakeholder expectations and requirements (4.1–4.2), and selected design-solution passages (4.4), were checked. The [Product Realization](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/5-0-product-realization/) verification, validation and transition passages (5.3–5.5), and [Technical Management](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/6-0-crosscutting-technical-management/) interface and configuration passages (6.3 and 6.5), were also inspected. The new common block assigns progressive reading beyond these inspected excerpts, including implementation and integration in 5.1–5.2; it does not claim a complete chapter audit or performed reader exercises. Its paper-card examples and calculations are original teaching constructions, not NASA experiments or requirements. The book's organizational and flight-unit procedures retain their own context.


**Fourth engineering-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** selected official [5.1 implementation](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/5-1-product-implementation/) passages on realization choices and controlled design inputs, and [5.2 integration](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/5-2-product-integration/) passages on interfaces and subsystem/environment interactions, were inspected. These support progressive common reading and the distinction between a response calculation and evidence about a realized configuration. The original axial-member evidence exercise is not a NASA test or requirement. Full chapters, institution-specific procedures and qualification activity are not claimed completed. Previous handbook identity, inspected passages and third-party-rights qualifications remain; source media stay external.


**Eighth engineering-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** read selected actual official [5.5 Product Transition](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/5-5-product-transition/) opening and input/preparation passages, including product identity and documentation, enabling products, personnel and site readiness. The common service block uses these as context for a changed configuration and evidence for useful operation. It does not adopt flight-program terminology as a universal certification requirement or claim physical transition/commissioning. The existing 2016 Revision 2 handbook identity, external PDF, historical inspections and rights qualifications remain; the full handbook and remaining transition guidance are assigned reading rather than a new whole-source audit.

**Common people/operation inspection — 1 November 2026:** selected actual official [5.5 Product Transition](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/5-5-product-transition/) prose in 5.5.1.2.1–5.5.1.2.2, 5.5.1.2.4–5.5.1.2.5 and the opening output discussion in 5.5.1.3 was read. Preparation, receiving-site/personnel readiness, functional checks after installation and preservation of work products support the common handover comparison. The paper-board investigation is original and supplies no NASA experiment or operational certification. Existing handbook identity, rights qualifications and broader assignments remain unchanged; no new whole-source audit is claimed.

